= Device driver =

== Overview ==
{{:Device driver/Simple}}

A '''device driver''' is software that mediates operations between a device or device class and a software environment. It implements an interface and device-specific control, translating requests into operations that the hardware, virtual device or controller can execute. Drivers may run in kernel space, user space or embedded environments. A driver need not be a separately loadable kernel module, and not all drivers control a physical peripheral.<ref name="linuxcore">Linux kernel documentation, [https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/driver-model/driver.html Device Drivers] and [https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/driver-model/binding.html Driver Binding], current documentation.</ref>

== Distinct roles ==
An [[Operating system]] manages processes, protection and resources; a driver implements a narrower interface for a device or class of devices. [[Computer architecture]] specifies relevant hardware and programmer-visible constraints. A bootloader's main role is transferring execution to another stage, although it may incorporate drivers. Firmware executes on a device or platform controller and may provide the protocol a host driver uses. None of these roles is universally identical.

== A request's journey ==
An application or kernel subsystem submits an operation through an interface. The driver validates and schedules it, maps buffers or programs registers as appropriate, then reports completion or error. A device may signal completion by interrupt, a polled status change or a queue entry. Some devices transfer data by DMA; others do not. Completion, memory ordering, error propagation and cancellation are part of correctness, not optional performance details.<ref name="linuxdma">Linux kernel documentation, [https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/dma-api-howto.html DMA API HOWTO], device DMA mapping and synchronization contracts.</ref>

== Discovery and lifetime ==
A bus or platform subsystem can discover a device and match it to a driver. The Linux driver model describes matching, probing, binding, removal and power-management callbacks; probe can fail or defer until dependencies are available.<ref name="linuxcore"/> Hot-unplug and failed initialization require safe cleanup of requests, resources and references. A driver must not continue using memory or hardware that has been removed.

== Implementation families ==
Windows Driver Frameworks provide both kernel-mode (KMDF) and user-mode (UMDF) driver models, with framework-managed object lifetimes and request queues. Linux has bus-specific driver APIs and generic driver-core infrastructure. These are platform-specific implementation models, not a single portable binary interface.<ref name="wdfcore">Microsoft Learn, [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/wdf/using-the-framework-to-develop-a-driver Using WDF to Develop a Driver], driver objects, device objects and I/O queues.</ref>

== Security and verification ==
Drivers may access privileged memory, DMA-capable devices and security-sensitive state. Threat models include malformed device responses, compromised firmware, malicious peripherals, race conditions, use-after-free and erroneous DMA mappings. User-mode placement can limit some consequences, but isolation depends on OS policies, hardware support and exposed interfaces. Testing should cover success and failure paths, concurrency, suspend/resume, removal and resource exhaustion.

The related [[Related to::Operating system|operating-system]], [[Related to::Computer architecture|computer-architecture]] and [[Related to::Computer security|security]] articles describe broader contexts. These links indicate association, not a claim that one subject subsumes the others.

